# Prefer functions over monolithic main

Structure code as small, named functions. Keep entrypoints thin: parse args, call helpers, exit. Do not put the whole program in the top-level script body or a single large `main`.

## Do

- Extract steps into named functions (resolve, validate, transform, write, …)
- Keep `main` / `__main__` / CLI dispatch to orchestration only
- Prefer reusable helpers over copy-pasted blocks in the entrypoint

## Don't

- Long imperative scripts with all logic at file top level
- One giant `main` that contains every branch and side effect
- Structural or behavior changes just to “use functions” — refactor style only unless asked

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — work at top level
need git
src=...
cp "$src" "$dest"

# ✅ GOOD — thin main
resolve_src() { ... }
install_bp() { ... }
main() {
  need git
  resolve_src
  install_bp
}
main "$@"
```

```python
# ❌ BAD
if __name__ == "__main__":
    # dozens of lines of logic here

# ✅ GOOD
def main() -> None:
    path = find_tool()
    run(path, parse_args())

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

Applies to shell, Python, and other languages in this repo. Same behavior; clearer structure.
